   "is_new": true,
   "source": {
    "label": "thm:star_graph_main",
    "number": "Theorem 3.1",
    "quote": "Suppose that the inequality $\\mathbb{E}[\\ell^2_t(i)] \\leq \\rho$ holds for all $t \\leq T$ and all $i \\in V$, for some $\\rho$ and $\\beta \\geq \\frac{1}{\\tau}$. Then, for any action $a \\in V$, Algorithm~\\ref{alg:star_graph_main} admits the following guarantee: ... \\leq \\frac{\\log{|V|}}{\\eta} + T\\eta\\tau\\rho + T\\beta. Furthermore, the algorithm does not switch more than $2\\nicefrac{T}{\\tau}$ times, in expectation."
   },
   "confidence": "medium",
   "notes": "Bound on loss regret excluding switching cost; expected number of switches separately bounded by 2T/tau. With eta=T^{-2/3}, tau=T^{2/3}, beta=T^{-1/3} the paper says total regret is O~(T^{2/3})."
  },
